---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Sensor from '../components/Sensor.astro';
import CognitiveStack from '../components/diagrams/CognitiveStack.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Cognitive Architecture — AnimaOS"
  description="The cognitive layer that sits above the somatic substrate: hierarchical control, embodied state modulation, selective forgetting."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="08 — Cognitive Architecture"
    title="A brain inside the body the OS provides."
    description="AnimaOS is an agent-first operating system. The OS itself is the body: sensorium, motor system, and homeostatic signals. The cognitive layer sitting above this body is structured as a hierarchy of control — fast cheap policies handle most events; slow expensive deliberation is invoked when fast policies cannot resolve the situation, and escalation itself is a learned policy."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Thesis</span>
        <h2>Spend compute deliberately, the way an organism spends metabolic energy.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The cognitive layer's intelligence is not constant. Body state — thermal
          load, memory pressure, battery, GPU utilisation, latency, API cost —
          continuously modulates the thresholds at which expensive reasoning is
          invoked. The technical core is a learned memory controller derived from
          stateful KV-cache gating, which decides what the system remembers,
          compresses, and forgets across long-horizon operation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <CognitiveStack />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">Six design principles</span>
        <h2>Each stated as a constraint, not an aspiration.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-2">
        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">01</span>
          <h3>Hierarchical control with graceful degradation.</h3>
          <p>
            The system must remain functional when the most expensive cognitive
            components are unavailable. Network down, rate-limited, on battery,
            thermally throttled — the system continues to operate, just more
            reflexively. The deliberative layer is a luxury, not a requirement.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">02</span>
          <h3>Embodiment is not metaphor.</h3>
          <p>
            Body state is not logged for display purposes; it is a control input.
            Thermal load directly modifies the cost threshold for invoking the
            cortex. Battery state shifts the routing policy between local and API
            models. Memory pressure triggers consolidation. If a body-state
            signal does not modulate at least one policy, it does not belong in
            the architecture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">03</span>
          <h3>Selective forgetting is a feature.</h3>
          <p>
            Perfect recall is not a goal. The system must learn to forget —
            boilerplate, failed attempts, transient state — while retaining
            what is structurally important. This applies at every level: KV-cache
            eviction within an episode, episode summarisation across hours,
            identity-level memory across the lifetime of the installation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">04</span>
          <h3>Proposals, not conversations.</h3>
          <p>
            Subsystems within the cognitive layer do not chat with each other.
            They submit structured proposals to a central arbiter, which selects,
            suppresses, or combines them. This is enforced architecturally to
            prevent the runaway message-passing patterns that plague conventional
            multi-agent frameworks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">05</span>
          <h3>Falsifiable, not visionary.</h3>
          <p>
            Every claim in the architecture must be testable. "The system feels
            its body" is replaced with "policy P measurably changes when
            telemetry T crosses threshold X." If a feature cannot be falsified,
            it does not ship; it goes in the speculative addendum.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card cog">
          <span class="cog-num mono">06</span>
          <h3>The substrate is silicon, not wetware.</h3>
          <p>
            Where the biological inspiration suggests a structure that maps
            cleanly onto silicon, it is used. Where it does not, it is discarded.
            There is no obligation to have a hippocampus-equivalent, an
            amygdala-equivalent, or any other named anatomical structure.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="eyebrow">The body daemon</span>
        <h2><code>animad</code> — small, fast, reliable, trustworthy.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The body layer is everything the cognitive system can sense and
          everything it can affect. It is implemented as a privileged daemon
          written in Rust, communicating with the cognitive layer over a
          structured local IPC channel. The choice of Rust is deliberate: this
          layer must survive the cognitive layer crashing.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <Sensor name="Thermal" detail="Per-zone temperatures from /sys/class/thermal on Linux, ioreg on macOS. 1 Hz baseline, higher under stress." />
        <Sensor name="Compute" detail="CPU per-core, GPU utilisation, VRAM occupancy, load average, scheduler pressure (PSI)." />
        <Sensor name="Memory" detail="Resident memory, page cache behaviour, swap pressure, OOM proximity." />
        <Sensor name="Power" detail="Battery level, charging state, power draw if available, AC connection." />
        <Sensor name="Network" detail="Connection state, bandwidth, latency to anchor hosts, rate-limit headroom against known APIs." />
        <Sensor name="Filesystem" detail="Watched directories produce structured change events via inotify / fsevents / equivalent." />
        <Sensor name="User-presence" detail="Keyboard/mouse idle time, active window, foreground app, screen state. Gated by explicit consent at install." />
        <Sensor name="Financial" detail="Tracked spending against API providers, daily/monthly budgets, projected burn rate. An agent that cannot feel cost will not bound it." />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="card kv-card">
        <span class="badge badge-blue mono">kv-controller</span>
        <h2 style="margin-top:1rem;">The learned KV-cache controller.</h2>
        <p>
          Without selective forgetting at the attention layer, an agent-first OS
          suffers the same context degradation that plagues every current
          long-running agent. The <code>kv-controller</code> crate is a stateful
          gating policy, derived from prior work, that decides what the system
          remembers, compresses, and forgets across long-horizon operation.
        </p>
        <p>
          With it, the system can run for hours or days, retain what matters
          about the user and the task, and discard what does not.
        </p>
        <p class="muted small">
          Full delivery breakdown lives in <code>docs/07-implementation-plan.md</code> under <strong>Stage 5 — Cognitive Layer</strong>, which is the canonical reconciliation of this document with the milestone plan.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .section-head {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .section-head h2 { margin: 1rem 0; }
  .lede {
    color: var(--fg-2);
    font-size: 1.0625rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cog { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.75rem; }
  .cog-num { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 700; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .cog h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0.25rem 0; }
  .cog p { margin: 0; }

  .kv-card {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-2);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--bg-1), rgba(93, 209, 255, 0.04));
  }
</style>
